e)\n4. For N > 0, carry out = OR(generate, propagate) where generate = AND(xN, yN) and propagate = AND(partial_sum, carry_in)\n\nThe approach:\n- Build a mapping from wire name to the gate that produces it\n- For each zN where N > 0 and N < max: zN should be produced by an XOR gate\n- One of the inputs to this XOR gate should be a carry (from an OR gate or from the AND of bit 0)\n- The other input should be the partial sum (from an XOR of xN, yN)\n\nWait, let me think about this differently. The swaps are on the output wires of gates. So the gate structure is fine - the connections between gates are fine - but the names on the output wires are wrong.\n\nActually no - the output wire name determines which gate reads it as input. So if gate A outputs wire \"foo\" and gate B reads wire \"foo\" as input, they're connected. If we swap outputs so gate A now outputs \"bar\" and gate B outputs \"foo\", then:\n- Gate A still computes the same value but it goes to \"bar\" now\n- Gate B still computes the same value but it goes to \"foo\" now  \n- Any gate that was reading \"foo\" still reads \"foo\" but now gets gate B's output instead of gate A's\n\nSo the swap changes which gate's output feeds into downstream gates.\n\nLet me think about this problem from the perspective of checking structural correctness.\n\nFor the `findSwappedWires` function, I need to identify wires that are in the wrong position. The approach:\n\n1. Build a map: for each wire name, which gate produces it?\n2. For each bit position, check structural rules\n3. Identify violations and trace them to swapped pairs\n\nLet me define the structural rules more precisely:\n\n**Rule 1**: `z00` must be produced by `XOR(x00, y00)`\n\n**Rule 2**: For N > 0 (not the last bit), `zN` must be produced by an XOR gate where:\n- One input is the carry-in (output of an OR gate for N > 1, or AND(x00, y00) for N = 1)\n- The other input is a wire produced by `XOR(xN, yN)` (the partial sum)\n\n**Rule 3**: The last z-bit (z_max)