nd the swaps.\n\n**Algorithm**:\n\n1. Build the gate graph (output wire -> gate, and also input wire -> gates that consume it)\n\n2. Identify the \"correct\" structure by tracing from inputs:\n   - The gate XOR(x00, y00) should output z00\n   - The gate AND(x00, y00) outputs carry0\n   - For bit N: XOR(xN, yN) outputs partial_sum_N\n   - XOR(partial_sum_N, carry_(N-1)) should output zN\n   - AND(xN, yN) outputs generate_N\n   - AND(partial_sum_N, carry_(N-1)) outputs propagate_N\n   - OR(generate_N, propagate_N) outputs carry_N\n   - carry_(max) = z_max\n\n3. Compare the expected output wire names with actual output wire names. Where they differ, those are the swapped wires.\n\nThe challenge is identifying which gate is which in the actual circuit, since the output wire names have been swapped.\n\nLet me think about how to identify gates despite swaps.\n\n**Gate identification by inputs**:\n- The gate with inputs x00 and y00 and op XOR should output z00 (but may output something else due to swap)\n- The gate with inputs x00 and y00 and op AND outputs carry0 (but may output something else)\n- For bit N: the gate with inputs xN and yN and op XOR outputs partial_sum_N\n- etc.\n\nThe inputs to gates are NOT swapped - only output wires are swapped. So I can identify gates by their inputs and operation.\n\nWait, but the inputs might also be wrong because a swap changes which wire feeds into downstream gates. No wait - the swap only exchanges the `out` fields of two gates. The `in1` and `in2` fields remain the same. So the actual wiring between gates doesn't change - only the labels on the output wires change.\n\nHmm, but that means if gate A outputs wire \"foo\" and that wire feeds into gate B as input, after swapping gate A's output to \"bar\", gate B no longer receives gate A's output. Instead, the gate that now outputs \"foo\" (due to the swap) feeds into gate B.\n\nSo the functional connectivity DOES change. The circuit's actual behavior changes because the wire names