he output wire names are different, so different gates produce different wires.\n\nBut the key thing is: the gate objects tell us exactly what each gate computes and what wires it reads. We just need to figure out the correct output labels.\n\nOK here's another approach. Let me think about what constraints the correct output labels must satisfy.\n\nIn the correct adder:\n- For bit 0: XOR(x0, y0) → z00, AND(x0, y0) → carry_0\n- For bit i >= 1: \n  - XOR(x_i, y_i) → some_internal_wire (partial sum)\n  - XOR(partial_sum, carry_{i-1}) → z_i\n  - AND(x_i, y_i) → some_internal_wire (generate)\n  - AND(partial_sum, carry_{i-1}) → some_internal_wire (propagate)\n  - OR(generate, propagate) → carry_i\n\nThe constraint is: z_i must be the output of the second XOR gate (XOR of partial sum and carry). This means:\n- z_i is produced by an XOR gate\n- One input of this XOR gate is the output of the partial sum XOR gate\n- The other input is a carry wire\n\nAnd for the carry chain:\n- carry_i is produced by an OR gate\n- One input is the output of the generate AND gate\n- The other input is the output of the propagate AND gate\n\nNow, in the swapped circuit, we know the gate objects. We can check:\n1. Is z_i produced by an XOR gate? If not, z_i is swapped with some other wire.\n2. If z_i IS produced by XOR, is one of its inputs the output of the partial sum XOR gate for bit i? (We can identify the partial sum gate because it has x_i, y_i as inputs.)\n   - We know the output wire name of the partial sum gate (even if it's swapped).\n   - Check if one of the inputs to the z_i gate matches this output wire name.\n\nThis is a good approach! Let me formalize it:\n\nFor each bit i >= 1:\n- ps_gate_i = the gate with op=XOR, in1=x_i, in2=y_i (or vice versa)\n- ps_output_i = ps_gate_i.out (the output wire name of the partial sum gate)\n- The gate that SHOULD produce z_i has op=XOR and one of its inputs should be ps_output_i\n\nSo check: is the gate with out=z_i an XOR gate with one input e