gate produces it, I find the gate with out=\"abc\". This gate might not be the same gate that produced \"abc\" in the original circuit (due to swaps). But it's the gate that produces \"abc\" in the swapped circuit.\n\nFor tracing the carry chain, I need to find the gate that takes the partial sum and carry as inputs. In the swapped circuit, the partial sum is produced by... hmm, which gate produces the partial sum? It's the XOR(x_i, y_i) gate, but its output name might be swapped.\n\nOK I think I need to just code this up and handle the complexity programmatically. Let me define the algorithm more precisely:\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. Build a map: wire_name → producing_gate (using the swapped out fields)\n2. Build a map: gate → [list of gates that take its output as input]\n   - For each gate G with out=w, find all gates H where H.in1 == w or H.in2 == w\n3. For each bit i, identify:\n   - ps_gate_i = gate with op=XOR and (in1,in2) = (x_i, y_i) [or (y_i, x_i)]\n   - gen_gate_i = gate with op=AND and (in1,in2) = (x_i, y_i)\n4. Determine which gate produces each z-wire:\n   - z_gate_i = gate with out=z_i (from the map in step 1)\n5. Check for swaps:\n   - For i = 0: ps_gate_0 should produce z00. If ps_gate_0.out != z00, there's a swap.\n     - Actually, this check is valid! ps_gate_0 (XOR(x0, y0)) should output z00. If it doesn't, z00 is swapped with ps_gate_0.out.\n   - For i >= 1: z_i should be produced by an XOR gate. Check if z_gate_i.op == XOR.\n     - If not, z_i is on the wrong gate.\n   - For i = N+1: z_{N+1} should be produced by an OR gate. Check if z_gate_{N+1}.op == OR.\n     - If not, z_{N+1} is on the wrong gate.\n\nWait, step 5 for i=0 is clever! In the original adder, XOR(x0, y0) → z00. So the gate with op=XOR, in1=x00, in2=y00 should have out=z00. If it doesn't, the output is swapped.\n\nMore generally: for bit i, the partial sum gate XOR(x_i, y_i) should output some specific wire. For i=0, it should be z00. For i >= 1, it should be an internal wire that feeds into the z_i gate.\n\nI can check: for i=0, does ps_gate_0.out == \"z00\"? If not, it's swapped.\n\nAnd: for i >= 1, ps_gate_i.out should NOT be a z-wire (because partial sums for i >= 1 are internal wires). If ps_gate_i.out is a z-wire, it's swapped.\n\nSimilarly, gen_gate_i.out should NOT be a z-wire (for any i). If it is, it's swapped.\n\nAnd carry_gate_i.out should NOT be a z-wire (except for the final carry, which IS z_{N+1}). If it is, it's swapped.\n\nSo I have several structural checks:\n\nCheck A: ps_gate_0.out should be z00\nCheck B: For i >= 1, ps_gate_i.out should NOT be a z-wire\nCheck C: For any i, gen_gate_i.out should NOT be a z-wire\nCheck D: For i >= 1, z_gate_i.op should be XOR\nCheck E: z_0.op should be XOR (same as Check A, really)\nCheck F: z_{N+1}.op should be OR\n\nThese checks can identify some swapped wires. But they might not catch all 8.\n\nLet me think about what other checks I can do.\n\nCheck G: For i >= 1, ps_gate_i.out should be an input to an XOR gate and an AND gate (the z_i gate and propagate gate). If it's not, it's swapped.\nCheck H: For i >= 1, gen_gate_i.out should be an input to an OR gate (the carry gate). If it's not, it's swapped.\n\nWait, Check G is interesting. Let me think about it.\n\nps_gate_i.out is the output wire name of the partial sum gate in the swapped circuit. I can find which gates take this wire as input (using the map from step 2).\n\nIn a correct adder, the partial sum for bit i feeds into:\n- The z_i gate (XOR)\n- The propagate gate (AND)\n\nIn the swapped circuit, ps_gate_i.out might be a swapped name. If it's swapped with, say, a generate output, then ps_gate_i.out would be a generate wire name, and it would feed into the carry gate (OR) instead of the z_i gate and propagate gate.\n\nSo I can check: does ps_gate_i.out feed into an XOR gate and an AND gate? If not, it's likely swapped.\n\nSimilarly, gen_gate_i.out should feed into an OR gate. If it feeds into an XOR or AND gate, it's likely swapped.\n\nThese are additional structural checks that can help identify swaps.\n\nOK, I think I have enough ideas. Let me implement the algorithm.\n\nLet me also think about the simulation-based approach as a complementary method.\n\nFor simulation: I can simulate the circuit with specific inputs and check if Z = X + Y. If not, I can identify which bits are wrong and trace back to the swaps.\n\nFor example:\n- Set all x_i = 0, y_i = 0. Then Z should be 0.\n- Set x_0 = 1, all others 0. Then Z should be 1 (z00 = 1, all others 0).\n- Set x_1 = 1, all others 0. Then Z should be 2 (z01 = 1, all others 0).\n- Etc.\n\nBut carry chain errors can propagate, making it hard to isolate the swap from simulation alone.\n\nA better simulation approach:\n- Set X = 2^i, Y = 2^i for various i. Then Z should be 2^{i+1}. This tests the carry from bit i.\n- If the carry from bit i is wrong, it could be due to a swap in bit i's logic.\n\nBut this still has the carry propagation issue.\n\nI think the structural approach is more reliable. Let me implement it.\n\nHere's my final algorithm:\n\n1. Build necessary data structures (maps).\n2. Identify all z-wires and their producing gates.\n3. Identify partial sum and generate gates for each bit.\n4. Check for obvious swaps (z-wires on wrong gate types, partial sum/generate outputs that are z-wires).\n5. For non-obvious swaps, trace the carry chain and check for consistency.\n6. Return the 8 swapped wires.\n\nLet me implement this now.\n\nFor the carry chain tracing, I'll use the following approach:\n- Start from bit 0.\n- carry_0 is produced by gen_gate_0 (AND(x0, y0)).\n- For bit i >= 1:\n  - ps_gate_i.out feeds into some gates. Find the XOR and AND gates that take it as input.\n  - One of these should be the z_i gate (XOR) and one the propagate gate (AND).\n  - But due to swaps, ps_gate_i.out might not feed into the correct gates.\n  - The z_i gate should have one input = ps_gate_i.out and another input = carry_{i-1}'s output.\n  - But carry_{i-1}'s output might also be swapped...\n\nOK I think the practical approach is:\n\n1. Do the easy structural checks first.\n2. For any remaining unidentified swaps, use a more sophisticated method.\n\nLet me also consider: what if I use a simulation-based approach with specific inputs to narrow down the swaps?\n\nFor example:\n- Set X = 0, Y = 0. All z-wires should be 0. Any z-wire that's 1 is wrong.\n- But due to swaps, some gates might produce wrong outputs even with all-zero inputs.\n\nWait, with all-zero inputs, all x_i = 0 and y_i = 0. Then:\n- XOR(0, 0) = 0\n- AND(0, 0) = 0\n- OR(0, 0) = 0\n\nAll gates produce 0. So all wires are 0, and Z = 0. This doesn't help.\n\nWhat about X = 1 (x00 = 1, all others 0), Y = 0?\n- z00 should be XOR(1, 0) = 1\n- carry_0 should be AND(1, 0) = 0\n- All higher bits should be 0 (since carry_0 = 0)\n\nSo Z should be 1. If z00 ≠ 1 or any z_i (i >= 1) ≠ 0, there's an error.\n\nBut this might not catch all swaps. For example, if two internal wires are swapped (and both z-wires are on correct gates), the simulation might still produce the correct output for this particular input.\n\nI think the most effective approach is to combine structural checks with simulation. But let me start with the structural approach and see how far it gets.\n\nLet me now actually write the code.\n\nHere's my implementation plan:\n\n```typescript\ninterface Gate {\n  op: 'AND' | 'OR' | 'XOR';\n  in1: string;\n  in2: string;\n  out: string;\n}\n\nfunction simulate(gates: Gate[], inputs: Record<string, number>): Record<string, number> {\n  const wires: Record<string, number> = { ...inputs };\n  const remaining = [...gates];\n  \n  while (remaining.length > 0) {\n    let progress = false;\n    for (let i = remaining.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n      const gate = remaining[i];\n      if (wires[gate.in1] !== undefined && wires[gate.in2] !== undefined) {\n        wires[gate.out] = applyOp(gate.op, wires[gate.in1], wires[gate.in2]);\n        remaining.splice(i, 1);\n        progress = true;\n      }\n    }\n    if (!progress) throw new Error(\"Circular dependency\");\n  }\n  \n  return wires;\n}\n\nfunction applyOp(op: string, a: number, b: number): number {\n  switch (op) {\n    case 'AND': return a & b;\n    case 'OR': return a | b;\n    case 'XOR': return a ^ b;\n    default: throw new Error(`Unknown op: ${op}`);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor `findSwappedWires`, let me implement the structural analysis:\n\n```typescript\nfunction findSwappedWires(gates: Gate[]): string[] {\n  // Step 1: Build maps\n  const outToGate = new Map<string, Gate>();\n  for (const gate of gates) {\n    outToGate.set(gate.out, gate);\n  }\n  \n  // Step 2: Identify z-wires and determine N\n  const zWires: string[] = [];\n  for (const gate of gates) {\n    if (gate.out.match(/^z\\d+$/)) {\n      zWires.push(gate.out);\n    }\n  }\n  // Sort z-wires by bit number\n  zWires.sort((a, b) => parseInt(a.slice(1)) - parseInt(b.slice(1)));\n  const maxZ = parseInt(zWires[zWires.length - 1].slice(1));\n  const N = maxZ - 1; // z_{N+1} is the highest, so N input bits... wait\n  // Actually, if z goes from z00 to z_{N+1}, then there are N+1 input bits (x00 to xNN, y00 to yNN)\n  // But wait, the problem says z00 to z(N+1), with inputs x00 to xNN and y00 to yNN\n  // So N+2 z-wires (z00 to z_{N+1}) and N+1 input pairs\n  // Hmm, let me re-read...\n  // \"inputs x00..xNN and y00..yNN, and outputs z00..z(N+1)\"\n  // So x goes from 00 to NN, that's N+1 input bits\n  // z goes from 00 to N+1, that's N+2 output bits\n  // The extra bit is for the carry\n  // For a 4-bit adder: x00-x03, y00-y03, z00-z04 (5 output bits for 4 input bits + carry)\n  // So N = 3 (max input bit index), and z goes from z00 to z04 = z_{N+1}\n  \n  // Let me determine N from the z-wires\n  // If max z index is M, then there are M input bits (x00 to x{M-1})\n  // Actually, from the example: 4-bit adder has x00-x03, y00-y03, z00-z04\n  // So N+1 = 4 input bits, N+2 = 5 output bits\n  // maxZ = 4 = N+1, so N = maxZ - 1\n  \n  const numBits = maxZ; // number of input bits (0 to numBits-1)\n  \n  // Step 3: For each bit i, identify partial sum and generate gates\n  const swappedWires: Set<string> = new Set();\n  \n  for (let i = 0; i < numBits; i++) {\n    const xi = `x${String(i).padStart(2, '0')}`;\n    const yi = `y${String(i).padStart(2, '0')}`;\n    \n    // Find XOR(xi, yi) gate — partial sum\n    // Find AND(xi, yi) gate — generate\n    const psGate = gates.find(g => g.op === 'XOR' && \n      ((g.in1 === xi && g.in2 === yi) || (g.in1 === yi && g.in2 === xi)));\n    const genGate = gates.find(g => g.op === 'AND' && \n      ((g.in1 === xi && g.in2 === yi) || (g.in1 === yi && g.in2 === xi)));\n    \n    if (!psGate || !genGate) continue;\n    \n    if (i === 0) {\n      // ps_gate_0 should output z00\n      if (psGate.out !== 'z00') {\n        swappedWires.add(psGate.out);\n        swappedWires.add('z00');\n      }\n    } else {\n      // ps_gate_i should NOT output a z-wire\n      if (psGate.out.match(/^z\\d+$/)) {\n        swappedWires.add(psGate.out);\n      }\n      // gen_gate_i should NOT output a z-wire\n      if (genGate.out.match(/^z\\d+$/)) {\n        swappedWires.add(genGate.out);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Step 4: Check z-wire gate types\n  for (let i = 1; i <= maxZ; i++) {\n    const zi = `z${String(i).padStart(2, '0')}`;\n    const gate = outToGate.get(zi);\n    if (!gate) continue;\n    \n    if (i < maxZ) {\n      // z_i (i >= 1, not the final carry) should be from XOR\n      if (gate.op !== 'XOR') {\n        swappedWires.add(zi);\n      }\n    } else {\n      // z_{maxZ} (final carry) should be from OR\n      if (gate.op !== 'OR') {\n        swappedWires.add(zi);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Step 5: More checks...\n  // Check if partial sum outputs feed into correct gate types\n  for (let i = 1; i < numBits; i++) {\n    const xi = `x${String(i).padStart(2, '0')}`;\n    const yi = `y${String(i).padStart(2, '0')}`;\n    \n    const psGate = gates.