swaps, these wires carry different values. So the simulation might give unexpected results.\n\nWait, I need to be more precise about what \"simulation\" means. The simulate function takes the gate array as given (with swapped outputs) and computes wire values based on the gate connections as described. The gate connections are:\n- Gate G: op, in1, in2, out\n- G reads from wires in1 and in2, computes op(in1, in2), and writes to wire out\n\nSo the simulation follows the actual circuit as described, including swaps. The result is the actual computation of the swapped circuit.\n\nFor the test xi=1, yi=0:\n- The simulation computes the actual values of all wires in the swapped circuit\n- The z-outputs might be wrong\n- But I can compare with the expected result (zi=1, all others 0)\n\nIf the z-outputs are wrong, I know which z-bits are affected. Then I can investigate further.\n\nOK let me just implement a clean, working solution. Here's my plan:\n\n1. `simulate`: topological sort / iterative evaluation. (Already done, straightforward.)\n2. `findSwappedWires`:\n   a. Use structural checks to identify obviously swapped z-wires.\n   b. Use simulation with test vectors to find wrong z-bits.\n   c. For each wrong z-bit, identify candidate swap partners.\n   d. Try swaps and verify.\n\nLet me implement this now, writing clean code.\n\nFor step 2c, identifying candidate swap partners for a wrong z-bit zi:\n- The gate currently outputting zi has the wrong structure for zi. Its output (zi) should be the output of a different gate.\n- The gate that SHOULD output zi has the correct structure but outputs some other wire w.\n- So zi and w are swapped.\n\nTo find w: I need to find the gate that has the correct structure for zi. For zi (0 < i < N), this is an XOR gate where one input comes from XOR(xi, yi). I can find this by:\n1. Finding the XOR(xi, yi) gate. Its output wire is the partial sum.\n2. Finding the gate that takes this partial sum as input and has op XOR. This gate should