uld be z01.\n4. Gate Z.out = \"gen01\" != \"z01\". So \"gen01\" and \"z01\" are swapped!\n\nThis works! The key is:\n- Find the XOR(xi, yi) gate and note its output wire name.\n- Find the XOR gate that takes this partial sum as one input.\n- This gate should output zi. Its actual output is the swapped wire.\n\nBut what if the XOR(xi, yi) gate's output is also swapped? Let's say ps01 is swapped with some other wire \"abc\".\n\nAfter swapping ps01 and abc:\n- Gate P: XOR(x1, y1), out=\"abc\" (was \"ps01\")\n- Some other gate: out=\"ps01\" (was \"abc\")\n\nNow, the gate that should compute z01 has in1=\"ps01\" and in2=\"c00\". But \"ps01\" now carries a different value (from the other gate). So this gate computes XOR of a wrong value and the carry, giving a wrong result.\n\nWhen I look for the XOR(xi, yi) gate, I find Gate P with out=\"abc\". The partial sum wire is \"abc\".\n\nI look for gates that take \"abc\" as input. But the z01 gate has in1=\"ps01\" (not \"abc\"). So I can't find it by searching for \"abc\".\n\nThis is the problem again. The partial sum wire name has changed, and the downstream gates still reference the old name.\n\nSo this approach only works when the partial sum wire is NOT swapped. If the partial sum wire is swapped, I can't find the z-gate this way.\n\nHmm. So the approach of finding the z-gate by following the partial sum wire works only when the partial sum wire is not swapped. In that case, I can identify the swap.\n\nBut what if the partial sum wire IS swapped? Then I need a different approach.\n\nLet me think about how to handle this. If the partial sum wire ps_i is swapped with some other wire w:\n- The XOR(xi, yi) gate outputs w (instead of ps_i)\n- Some other gate outputs ps_i (instead of w)\n- The z-gate still takes ps_i as input (but now gets a wrong value)\n\nTo detect this, I can check: does the gate outputting z_i have the correct inputs? If the z-gate has inputs (ps_i, carry_i), and ps_i now carries a wrong value, the z-gate still produces a wrong result. But structurally, the z-gate looks correct (XOR with inputs ps_i and carry_i).\n\nSo I can't detect this swap by looking at the z-gate alone. I need to check the gate outputting ps_i and see if it's the correct XOR(xi, yi) gate.\n\nWait, I CAN check this! I can trace backward from the z-gate's inputs:\n- z-gate has inputs ps_i and carry_i.\n- Find the gate outputting ps_i. Is it XOR(xi, yi)? If not, ps_i is swapped.\n- Find the gate outputting carry_i. Is it a carry gate (OR or AND(x0,y0))? If not, carry_i is swapped.\n\nThis backward tracing works because the gate outputting ps_i is determined by the out field (after swaps). If ps_i is swapped, the gate outputting ps_i is NOT XOR(xi, yi), and I can detect that.\n\nBut wait, what if ps_i is swapped with another partial sum wire ps_j? Then the gate outputting ps_i is XOR(xj, yj), which IS an XOR gate but for the wrong bit. I need to check if it's XOR(xi, yi) specifically.\n\nSo the backward trace checks:\n1. Find gate G outputting ps_i.\n2. Check: is G.op == 'XOR' and are G's inputs xi and yi?\n3. If not, ps_i is swapped.\n\nAnd for the carry wire:\n1. Find gate G outputting carry_i.\n2. Check: is G a carry gate? (OR for bits >= 1, AND(x0, y0) for bit 0's carry)\n3. If not, carry_i is swapped.\n\nThis is a good approach! Let me combine it with the forward approach.\n\n**Combined approach**:\n\n1. For each z-wire zi, check the gate producing it.\n   a. If op is wrong, zi is swapped.\n   b. If op is correct, trace back inputs:\n      - One input should come from XOR(xi, yi). Check by finding the producing gate.\n      - The other input should come from a carry gate. Check by finding the producing gate.\n   c. If input traces are wrong, zi is affected by a swap (but might not be directly swapped).\n\n2. For each gate that should output a z-wire (determined by structure), check if its output matches.\n   a. XOR(x0, y0) should output z00.\n   b. XOR gate with one input from XOR(xi, yi) should output zi.\n   c. OR gate that's the last carry should output zN.\n\n3. For internal wires, use carry chain consistency checks.\n\nLet me implement this now. I think the approach is sound, but I need to handle the cases where backward traces fail gracefully.\n\nActually, let me simplify even further. Let me implement just the following checks:\n\n**Check A**: For each z-wire, check if the producing gate has the correct op.\n- z00: XOR\n- zi (0 < i < N): XOR\n- zN: OR (for N >= 1)\n\n**Check B**: For each z-wire zi (0 < i < N) from an XOR gate, check if one input comes from XOR(xi, yi).\n- Trace back: find gate outputting z-gate's input. Check if it's XOR(xi, yi).\n\n**Check C**: For z00, check if the producing gate's inputs are x00 and y00.\n\n**Check D**: For zN (from an OR gate), check if both inputs come from AND gates.\n\n**Check E**: For each non-z XOR gate with one input from XOR(xi, yi), its output should be zi. If it's not, flag both.\n\n**Check F**: For XOR(x0, y0), its output should be z00. If it's not, flag both.\n\nThese checks should catch most swaps involving z-wires. For swaps involving only internal wires, I'll need simulation-based checks.\n\nLet me now write the code. I'll keep it clean and focused.\n\nActually wait, I realize there might be an even cleaner approach. Let me think about what the problem is really asking.\n\nThe problem says the circuit is a binary adder with 4 pairs of swapped output wires. The task is to find all 8 swapped wires.\n\nThe most robust approach: **simulate the circuit with several test vectors, compare with expected X+Y=Z, and use the errors to identify swapped wires**.\n\nHere's a concrete algorithm:\n\n1. Simulate with test vectors and find which z-bits are wrong.\n2. For each wrong z-bit, the z-wire is likely swapped (or an internal wire in its carry chain is swapped).\n3. Apply the swaps we're confident about (from structural checks).\n4. Re-simulate and repeat.\n\nBut step 2 is imprecise. A wrong z-bit doesn't necessarily mean the z-wire itself is swapped; it could be an internal wire.\n\nLet me think about a different approach: **for each pair of output wires, test if swapping them makes the circuit more correct**.\n\nWith ~220 gates, there are ~220 output wires. The number of pairs is ~24000. For each pair, we simulate with a few test vectors and check if the result improves. This takes ~24000 * 5 * 220 ≈ 26 million operations. That's very fast.\n\nBut \"more correct\" is ambiguous. A wrong swap could fix some test cases while breaking others. And we need to find 4 pairs, not just 1.\n\nHowever, if we use a GREEDY approach (find the swap that fixes the most test cases, apply it, repeat), it might work.\n\nActually, a better approach: **find the swap that, when applied, results in the circuit passing ALL test cases for the affected bits**.\n\nLet me think about this more concretely.\n\nTest vectors: for each bit i (0 to N), test (xi=1, yi=0) and (xi=0, yi=1). Expected: zi=1, all others 0. Also test (xi=1, yi=1) for each i. Expected: z_{i+1}=1, all others 0.\n\nFor a correct swap (one of the 4 pairs), applying it should make some of these test cases pass that were previously failing.\n\nFor an incorrect swap, it might make some pass but break others.\n\nThe key insight: if we test with (xi=1, yi=0) for ALL bits, and a single swap fixes the result for bit i without breaking any other bits, it's almost certainly a correct swap.\n\nBut a single swap might not fix bit i completely (because other swaps also affect bit i). So we might need to apply multiple swaps before any test case passes.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about the time constraints.\n\n5 seconds for ~220 gates. That's plenty of time. Even O(n^3) algorithms should work.\n\nLet me go with a combined approach:\n1. Use structural checks to identify obvious swaps (involving z-wires with wrong gate types).\n2. Use simulation with test vectors to identify wrong z-bits.\n3. For each wrong z-bit, try all possible swap partners and test.\n\nFor step 3, the number of wrong z-bits is at most ~8 (since 4 swap pairs can affect at most 8 z-bits directly, plus some indirectly through carry chain corruption). For each wrong z-bit, the number of candidate swap partners is at most ~220. So the total number of swaps to try is ~8 * 220 = 1760. For each swap, we simulate with ~5 test vectors, each taking O(220). Total: 1760 * 5 * 220 ≈ 2 million operations. Very fast.\n\nBut we need to be smarter about which swap partners to try. Not all 220 wires are equally likely.\n\nOK, I think I've been overthinking this. Let me just implement a clean solution and test it. Here's my final plan:\n\n1. `simulate`: iterative evaluation (simple, correct).\n2. `findSwappedWires`:\n   a. Build gate-by-output map.\n   b. Check each z-wire for structural correctness (op type, input structure).\n   c. Check each gate for \"should be a z-output\" (output mismatch).\n   d. Trace the carry chain forward from bit 0, checking for mismatches.\n   e. Use simulation to find wrong z-bits and try swap candidates.\n\nLet me write the code now. I'll start with a clean implementation of both functions.\n\nFor the carry chain trace, I'll use the following approach:\n- For each bit, find the gates with x/y inputs (these are always findable).\n- For subsequent gates, search by input wire names (using the output wire names from previously found gates).\n- If a gate can't be found (because the input wire was swapped), note the break in the chain.\n- After the chain trace, collect all mismatches.\n\nWait, I just realized something important. When I find the XOR(xi, yi) gate, its output wire name is the ACTUAL wire name in the swapped circuit. Other gates that take this wire as input will have this wire name in their in1 or in2 fields. So I CAN find downstream gates by searching for this wire name.\n\nBut wait, the downstream gates' in1 and in2 fields are UNCHANGED by swaps. So they reference the ORIGINAL wire names (before swaps). If the XOR(xi, yi) gate's output was swapped from \"ps01\" to \"abc\", then:\n- The XOR(xi, yi) gate now has out=\"abc\"\n- Downstream gates have in1 or in2 = \"ps01\" (original name, unchanged)\n- These downstream gates now receive the value from whatever gate currently outputs \"ps01\" (which is the gate swapped with the XOR(xi, yi) gate)\n\nSo the downstream gates DON'T reference \"abc\". They still reference \"ps01\". This means I CAN'T find the downstream gates by searching for \"abc\" as an input.\n\nBut I CAN find them by searching for \"ps01\" as an input. And \"ps01\" is output by the gate that was swapped with the XOR(xi, yi) gate.\n\nSo the circuit connectivity is: [gate swapped with XOR(xi,yi)] outputs \"ps01\" -> downstream gates take \"ps01\" as input. And XOR(xi,yi) outputs \"abc\" -> whatever gate takes \"abc\" as input (which might be a gate in a different part of the circuit).\n\nThis means the carry chain, as connected in the swapped circuit, goes through different gates than in the correct circuit. The wire \"ps01\" now carries a different value, and the downstream gates get this wrong value.\n\nFor my tracing, I have two options:\n1. Trace the ORIGINAL carry chain (using original wire names). This requires knowing which wire names are \"original\" (i.e., which gate should output which wire).\n2. Trace the ACTUAL carry chain (following the swapped circuit connections). This gives the actual (wrong) computation.\n\nFor option 1, I don't know the original wire names (they're random internal names). So I can't do this directly.\n\nFor option 2, tracing the actual circuit gives the actual (wrong) computation, which doesn't help me find the correct structure.\n\nHmm, so the carry chain trace approach doesn't work as simply as I thought.\n\nLet me go back to the approach that I know works: **backward tracing from z-wires**.\n\nFor each z-wire zi:\n1. Find gate G with G.out == zi.\n2. Check G's structure (op, inputs).\n3. Trace back from G's inputs to verify they come from the correct sources.\n\nThe backward trace works because:\n- G.in1 and G.in2 are wire names that exist in the circuit.\n- The gate outputting G.in1 can be found from the gate-by-output map.\n- That gate's structure can be checked.\n\nSo for each z-wire zi, I can verify:\n- Is the gate producing zi the correct type?\n- Do its inputs trace back to the correct sources?\n\nThis gives me a set of \"suspicious\" z-wires and internal wires.\n\nThen, for each suspicious wire, I can find the correct gate for that role and identify the swap.\n\nLet me implement this now.\n\nActually, I just realized there's an even simpler approach that I've been missing:\n\n**Approach: For each gate, check if its output wire name is consistent with its role**\n\nA gate's role can be determined (at least partially) from its (op, in1, in2). The output wire name should be consistent with the role. If it's not, the wire is swapped.\n\nRoles and expected output types:\n1. XOR(x0, y0): should output z00 (a z-wire)\n2. AND(x0, y0): should output a carry wire (internal, used by bit 1)\n3. XOR(xi, yi) for i >= 1: should output a partial sum wire (internal)\n4. AND(xi, yi) for i >= 1: should output a generate wire (internal)\n5. XOR(partial_sum, carry): should output zi (a z-wire)\n6. AND(partial_sum, carry): should output a propagate wire (internal)\n7. OR(generate, propagate): should output a carry wire (internal) or zN (a z-wire)\n\nFor roles 1, 5, and 7 (when it's the last carry), the expected output is a specific z-wire. If the actual output doesn't match, we've found a swap.\n\nFor roles 2, 3, 4, 6, and 7 (when it's not the last carry), the expected output is an internal wire. We can't check the name directly, but we can check if the wire is used correctly by downstream gates.\n\nThe key checks are for roles 1, 5, and 7. Let me focus on these.\n\n**Role 1 check**: XOR(x0, y0) should output z00.\n- Find all XOR gates with inputs x00 and y00.\n- For each, check if out == z00.\n- If not, out and z00 are swapped.\n\n**Role 5 check**: XOR(partial_sum, carry) should output zi.\n- Find all XOR gates where one input comes from XOR(xi, yi).\n- For each, determine i from the partial sum source.\n- Check if out == zi.\n- If not, out and zi are swapped.\n\n**Role 7 check**: OR(generate, propagate) should output zN (for the last bit).\n- This is harder to check without knowing the carry chain.\n- But we can check: the gate outputting zN should be OR. If it's not, zN is swapped.\n- And any OR gate whose inputs come from AND gates and doesn't output a z-wire or an internal carry could be the last carry.\n\nLet me implement these checks. For role 5, I need to identify which XOR gates have one input from XOR(xi, yi). This requires backward tracing from the gate's inputs.\n\nHere's the algorithm:\n\n```typescript\nfunction findSwappedWires(gates: Gate[]): string[] {\n  const gateByOutput = new Map<string, Gate>();\n  for (const g of gates) gateByOutput.set(g.out, g);\n\n  // Determine N\n  let N = -1;\n  for (const g of gates) {\n    const m = g.out.match(/^z(\\d+)$/);\n    if (m) N = Math.max(N, parseInt(m[1]));\n  }\n\n  const swapped = new Set<string>();\n\n  // Check each z-wire\n  for (let i = 0; i <= N; i++) {\n    const zi = `z${String(i).padStart(2, '0')}`;\n    const g = gateByOutput.get(zi);\n    if (!g) { swapped.add(zi); continue; }\n\n    if (i === 0) {\n      // z00 should come from XOR(x00, y00)\n      if (g.op !== 'XOR' || !isXY(g, 'x00', 'y00')) {\n        swapped.add(zi);\n      }\n    } else if (i < N) {\n      // zi should come from XOR\n      if (g.op !== 'XOR') {\n        swapped.add(zi);\n      } else {\n        // Check if one input comes from XOR(xi, yi)\n        const xi = `x${String(i).padStart(2, '0')}`;\n        const yi = `y${String(i).padStart(2, '0')}`;\n        if (!inputFromXY(g, xi, yi, gateByOutput)) {\n          swapped.add(zi);\n        }\n      }\n    } else {\n      // zN should come from OR (for N >