o the structural trace needs to follow the actual wire names used in gate inputs.\n\nLet me reconsider:\n\nIn the CORRECT circuit:\n- AND(x00, y00) → c00\n- XOR(t01, c00) → z01\n\nAfter swapping c00 and abc:\n- AND(x00, y00) → abc (was c00)\n- XOR(t01, c00) → z01 (unchanged because this gate's inputs didn't change)\n\nWait, this can't be right. If AND(x00, y00) now outputs abc, then wire c00 doesn't have the carry value anymore. But gate XOR(t01, c00) still reads from c00. So what produces c00 now?\n\nSome other gate that originally output abc now outputs c00. That gate has some different operation and inputs.\n\nSo in this case:\n1. I find AND(x00, y00) → its output is abc\n2. I need to find the carry wire for bit 1. The carry for bit 1 should be the output of AND(x00, y00), which is abc.\n3. But the gate XOR(t01, c00) still reads from c00. So the actual circuit uses c00 (not abc) as input to the XOR gate for z01.\n4. c00 is now produced by a different gate (the one that originally output abc).\n\nSo the structural trace should follow the actual gate connections:\n- AND(x00, y00) → abc (but this is the CORRECT carry, just mislabeled)\n- The XOR gate for z01 reads from c00 (which is NOT the carry anymore)\n- So z01 gets the wrong input\n\nFor my trace, I need to:\n1. Find AND(x00, y00) → abc. This tells me the carry for bit 0 should be abc.\n2. But the XOR gate for z01 uses c00, not abc. So I need to look for XOR(t01, c00), not XOR(t01, abc).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about it differently.\n\nThe key insight: **the circuit's gate connections (in1, in2) define the actual data flow**. The output wire names just label the results. When outputs are swapped, the labels are wrong, but the data flow through the circuit is determined by the connections.\n\nWait no, that's wrong too. The data flow IS determined by wire names. If gate A outputs wire \"abc\" and gate B has in1=\"abc\", then B reads from A. If we swap A's output to \"xyz\" instead, then 