ps affecting carry wires):\n   - Trace the carry chain following actual gate connections\n   - Identify where the chain breaks\n\nActually, I think the MOST robust approach that works for all cases is:\n\n**Comprehensive simulation-based approach with bit isolation:**\n\n1. For each bit i (0 to N), simulate with x=2^i and y=0. Expected: z=2^i.\n   - If z ≠ 2^i, some z-bits are wrong. Record which z-bits differ.\n\n2. For each bit i, simulate with x=2^i and y=2^i. Expected: z=2^(i+1).\n   - This tests the carry propagation.\n\n3. From the wrong z-bits, identify the swapped z-wires.\n\n4. For each swapped z-wire, find the gate that SHOULD produce it but doesn't.\n\n5. The gate that should produce zi is determined by the adder structure:\n   - For i=0: XOR(x0, y0)\n   - For i>0: XOR(partial_sum_i, carry_i)\n\n6. If the gate that should produce zi has a different output, that output is the other wire in the swap.\n\nBut this still has the problem of identifying the partial_sum and carry wires when swaps corrupt the carry chain.\n\nOK, let me try a completely different, simpler approach that I've seen work for AoC 2024 Day 24:\n\n**Rule-based approach for checking each gate:**\n\nFor a correct adder:\n1. If a gate outputs z00, it must be XOR(x00, y00)\n2. If a gate outputs zN (highest z), it must be OR (carry out)\n3. If a gate outputs zi (0 < i < N), it must be XOR, and one of its inputs must come from XOR(xi, yi)\n4. If a gate is XOR(xi, yi) for i > 0, its output must NOT be a z-wire (it's the partial sum)\n5. If a gate is AND(xi, yi), its output must NOT be a z-wire (it's the generate)\n6. If a gate is XOR with one input from XOR(xi, yi), its output should be zi (or it feeds into the carry chain)\n\nThe violations of these rules identify swapped wires.\n\nLet me implement this as a set of rules:\n\nRule 1: z00 must come from XOR(x00, y00). If not, z00 is swapped with the actual output of XOR(x00, y00).\n\nRule 2: For i > 0 and i < N, if zi doesn't come from an XOR gate, zi is swapped. The wire it's swapped with is the output of the gate that should produce zi.\n\nRule 3: zN should come from OR(generate, propagate). If it comes from a different gate type, zN is swapped.\n\nRule 4: If a gate is XOR(xi, yi) for i > 0, its output should NOT be a z-wire. If it is, the output is swapped.\n\nRule 5: If a gate is AND(xi, yi), its output should NOT be a z-wire. If it is, the output is swapped.\n\nHmm, but these rules alone might not catch all swaps. Let me think about what swaps are possible...\n\nActually, I think there's an even simpler observation. Let me enumerate the possible roles for each gate output:\n\nIn a correct adder, each gate output is one of:\n- z00 (bit 0 sum)\n- carry_0 (bit 0 carry)\n- partial_sum_i (for each bit i, 1 ≤ i < N)\n- z_i (for each bit i, 1 ≤ i < N)\n- generate_i (for each bit i, 1 ≤ i < N)\n- propagate_i (for each bit i, 1 ≤ i < N)\n- carry_i (for each bit i, 1 ≤ i < N-1... well, carry_{N-1} = zN)\n\nEach of these has a specific structural pattern:\n- z00: XOR(x00, y00) — only gate with direct x0/y0 inputs producing z\n- carry_0: AND(x00, y00)\n- partial_sum_i: XOR(xi, yi) for i > 0\n- z_i: XOR(partial_sum_i, carry_{i-1}) for i > 0\n- generate_i: AND(xi, yi) for i > 0\n- propagate_i: AND(partial_sum_i, carry_{i-1}) for i > 0\n- carry_i: OR(generate_i, propagate_i) for i > 0\n\nNote that carry_{N-1} = zN.\n\nThe structural pattern uniquely identifies each gate's role (given the circuit is a valid adder). The output wire should match the role. If it doesn't, the wire is swapped.\n\nBut the challenge is that some roles have similar patterns. For example:\n- z_i: XOR(partial_sum_i, carry_{i-1})\n- partial_sum_j: XOR(xj, yj)\n\nBoth are XOR gates, but one has x/y inputs and the other has internal wire inputs. These can be distinguished by whether the inputs are x/y wires or internal wires.\n\nSimilarly:\n- carry_0: AND(x00, y00)\n- generate_i: AND(xi, yi) for i > 0\n- propagate_i: AND(partial_sum_i, carry_{i-1}